{"t":"2026-11-23T07:16:55.554Z","i":11,"do":"waitReply","expect":"a long answer streams in; it is readable, not cut off, and the buttons under it never cover the words","timeline":[{"at":160,"badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":true,"streaming":0},{"at":6744,"badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":false,"streaming":1},{"at":12282,"badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":false,"streaming":0}],"watchShots":[{"at":1704,"mark":1500,"shot":"21-long-wait-1.5s.png","badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":true,"streaming":0},{"at":3160,"mark":3000,"shot":"22-long-wait-3.0s.png","badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":true,"streaming":0},{"at":6089,"mark":6000,"shot":"23-long-wait-6.0s.png","badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":true,"streaming":0}],"firstReplyMs":6593,"newBubbles":1,"reactions":[],"ok":false,"duplicates":0,"lastReply":"Sheru the tiger lived in the Sundarbans and was tired of the same old dinner every night. One evening the smell of saffron and fried onions drifted over from a village wedding, and he followed it to the edge of the forest. There he watched an old cook named Rahim layer rice and spices in a huge pot, and Sheru decided he would learn to make this magic himself.\n\nHis first tries were a disaster. He burnt the rice, used a whole fistful of chillies, and sneezed so hard that the monkeys fell out of their trees laughing. But Sheru kept sneaking back each night to watch Rahim, learning to soak the rice, brown the onions slowly, and seal the pot with dough so the steam stayed in. Bit by bit, his paws grew steady and his patience grew longer.\n\nAt last, one full-moon night, Sheru lifted the lid and a perfect cloud of steam rose up, every grain long and fragrant. He invited the whole forest, and even the deer came, nervously at first, then for second helpings. From that day on nobody ran when Sheru walked by. They only asked, \"What's cooking tonight?\"\n\n12:46 PM","kit":[],"replyFaults":["reply does not match /biryani|बिरयानी/"],"ms":18301,"shot":"24-long.png"}
